: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Travis Davies , cite6†Yiqi Huang , cite7†Alexi Gladstone , cite8†Yunxin Liu , cite9†Xiang Chen , cite10†Heng Ji , cite11†Huxian Liu , cite12†Luhui Hu L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled EBT-Policy: Energy Unlocks Emergent Physical Reasoning Capabilities, by Travis Davies and 7 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite13†View PDF cite14†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Implicit policies parameterized by generative models, such as Diffusion Policy, have become the standard for policy learning and Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models in robotics. However, these approaches often suffer from high computational cost, exposure bias, and unstable inference dynamics, which lead to divergence under distribution shifts.\nL17: Energy-Based Models (EBMs) address these issues by learning energy landscapes end-to-end and modeling equilibrium dynamics, offering improved robustness and reduced exposure bias. Yet, policies parameterized by EBMs have historically struggled to scale effectively. Recent work on Energy-Based Transformers (EBTs) demonstrates the scalability of EBMs to high-dimensional spaces, but their potential for solving core challenges in physically embodied models remains underexplored.\nL18: We introduce a new energy-based architecture, EBT-Policy, that solves core issues in robotic and real-world settings. Across simulated and real-world tasks, EBT-Policy consistently outperforms diffusion-based policies, while requiring less training and inference computation. Remarkably, on some tasks it converges within just two inference steps, a 50x reduction compared to Diffusion Policy's 100.\nL19: Moreover, EBT-Policy exhibits emergent capabilities not seen in prior models, such as zero-shot recovery from failed action sequences using only behavior cloning and without explicit retry training. By leveraging its scalar energy for uncertainty-aware inference and dynamic compute allocation, EBT-Policy offers a promising path toward robust, generalizable robot behavior under distribution shifts.\nL20: Comments:  | 9 pages, 6 figures, 4 tables\nL21: Subjects:  | Robotics (cs.RO); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite15†arXiv:2510.27545 [cs.RO]\nL23:    | (or cite16†arXiv:2510.27545v1 [cs.RO] for this version)\nL24:    | cite17†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.27545†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Luhui Hu [cite18†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Fri, 31 Oct 2025 15:21:05 UTC (12,626 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled EBT-Policy: Energy Unlocks Emergent Physical Reasoning Capabilities, by Travis Davies and 7 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite13†View PDF L37:   * cite14†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite19†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite20†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.RO\nL44: \nL45: cite21†< prev |   cite22†next > L46: \nL47: cite23†new | cite24†recent | cite25†2025-10 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite26†cs L52: cite27†cs.AI L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite28†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite29†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite30†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL61: \nL62: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL63: \nL64: [Button: ×]\nL65: \nL66: loading...\nL67: \nL68: Data provided by:\nL69: \nL70: ### Bookmark\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL73: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL74: \nL75: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL76: \nL77: Bibliographic Explorer (cite31†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL78: \nL79: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL80: \nL81: Connected Papers (cite32†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL82: \nL83: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL84: \nL85: Litmaps (cite33†